        conceptEn: "RLS restricts visible rows with USING and permitted new values with WITH CHECK. The table owner normally bypasses RLS unless FORCE is enabled; superusers and roles with BYPASSRLS bypass it even with FORCE. The example takes tenant_id from a session setting: a trusted server must set it from verified identity inside each transaction, for example with set_config(..., true), so it does not leak to the next pool user. The setting is not proof of identity by itself: an untrusted client able to execute arbitrary SQL and change it can impersonate another tenant. Review the trust boundary, role privileges, and read/write policies as the actual application role, including missing context. Evaluate a tenant_id index against the plan and workload.",
